(()=>{'use strict';
const METHODS={"cn_001":["Cut the tofu into 2 cm cubes. Bring a saucepan of lightly salted water to a gentle simmer, slide in the tofu, and simmer for 1–2 minutes; drain carefully and keep warm.","Finely chop the fermented black beans, garlic, ginger, and scallions. Stir the cornstarch with a little cold water to make a smooth slurry and keep it nearby.","Heat a wok over medium-high heat. Add the ground pork or beef and cook for 3–4 minutes, breaking it into small crumbles, until the meat is fully cooked and beginning to brown.","Lower the heat slightly and add the doubanjiang, fermented black beans, garlic, and ginger. Stir-fry for about 1 minute, until the oil is red and the aromatics smell fragrant without scorching.","Pour in the stock and add the soy sauce and sugar. Bring the sauce to a gentle simmer and taste for salt before adding more seasoning.","Slide in the blanched tofu and simmer gently for 5–6 minutes, nudging the cubes with the wok rather than stirring aggressively so they stay intact.","Stir the cornstarch slurry again, then add it in two or three small additions while gently shaking the wok. Let each addition simmer briefly before deciding whether the sauce needs more thickening.","When the sauce is glossy and clings lightly to the tofu, remove the wok from the heat and sprinkle over ground Sichuan pepper.","Finish with scallions and serve immediately with hot rice."],"cn_002":["Cut the chicken thigh into small, even cubes. Toss with soy sauce, Shaoxing wine, and cornstarch and let it marinate for about 10 minutes.","Mix the Chinkiang vinegar, sugar, and a little soy sauce in a small bowl so the finishing sauce is ready before the wok gets hot.","Cut the dried chilies into short sections and shake out some seeds if you want less heat. Prepare the Sichuan peppercorns, garlic, ginger, scallions, and roasted peanuts.","Heat a wok over high heat. Stir-fry the chicken for 3–4 minutes, until browned on the outside and nearly cooked through, then transfer it to a plate.","Lower the heat to medium. Add the dried chilies and Sichuan peppercorns and stir for 15–30 seconds, just until fragrant; add the garlic and ginger and stir another 20 seconds.","Return the chicken to the wok and pour in the vinegar-sugar sauce. Toss over high heat for about 1 minute, until the chicken is fully cooked and the sauce becomes a shiny glaze.","Add the scallions and roasted peanuts and toss for 20–30 seconds so the peanuts stay crisp.","Serve immediately with hot rice."],"cn_003":["Place the pork belly in a saucepan with ginger, scallions, and Shaoxing wine. Cover with water, bring to a gentle simmer, and cook for 20–25 minutes, until the pork is cooked through but still firm enough to slice.","Remove the pork and let it cool until easy to handle. Pat the surface dry, then slice it as thinly as practical.","Prepare the doubanjiang, fermented black beans, sliced leek, green pepper, soy sauce, and sugar before heating the wok.","Heat a wok over medium-high heat and add the pork slices. Cook for 4–5 minutes, turning frequently, until some fat renders and the edges begin to curl and lightly brown.","Push the pork to the side of the wok. Add the doubanjiang and fermented black beans to the rendered fat and fry for 30–60 seconds, until fragrant and the oil turns red.","Toss the pork through the chile-bean mixture so every slice is coated.","Add the leek and green pepper and stir-fry for 1–2 minutes, until just tender but still lively in color.","Season with soy sauce and a small amount of sugar, toss another 30 seconds, and taste before adding more salt.","Serve immediately with rice."],"cn_004":["Cut the Chinese eggplant into batons. Toss with a little salt and let stand for 10 minutes, then blot thoroughly dry so the pieces brown rather than steam.","Mix the Chinkiang vinegar, sugar, soy sauce, and stock in a small bowl. Prepare the doubanjiang, garlic, ginger, and scallions separately.","Heat a wok over medium-high heat. Cook the eggplant in batches for 5–7 minutes total, turning so the surfaces brown and the centers become tender; transfer to a plate.","Lower the heat slightly and add the doubanjiang, garlic, and ginger. Stir-fry for 30–60 seconds, until fragrant and the oil is tinted red.","Pour in the prepared vinegar-sugar sauce and bring it to a brisk simmer.","Return the eggplant to the wok and toss gently for 2–3 minutes, until the sauce reduces and coats the tender pieces.","Taste and adjust the sweet-sour-salty balance, adding a splash of stock only if the sauce has reduced too far.","Finish with scallions and serve hot."],"cn_005":["Mix the sesame paste, chile seasoning, Chinkiang vinegar, soy sauce, garlic, and ground Sichuan pepper until smooth. If the paste is very thick, loosen it gradually with a spoonful of warm water.","Bring a large pot of water to a boil. While it heats, chop the ya cai and scallions and have the ground pork ready.","Heat a wok or skillet over medium-high heat and cook the pork for 4–5 minutes, breaking it into small crumbles, until fully cooked and lightly browned.","Add the ya cai and stir-fry for about 1 minute, until fragrant and any excess moisture has evaporated.","Cook the wheat noodles in the boiling water for 2–4 minutes, or according to thickness, until tender with a little bite.","Reserve a cup of noodle water, then drain the noodles well. Use a little of the hot noodle water to adjust each bowl of sesame-chile sauce to a creamy, pourable consistency.","Divide the noodles among the sauced bowls and top with the pork-ya cai mixture and scallions.","Mix thoroughly from the bottom before eating so every strand is coated."],"cn_006":["Slice the white fish fillet against the grain into thin, even pieces. Toss gently with egg white and cornstarch and refrigerate for about 15 minutes while you prepare the broth.","Cut the napa cabbage into bite-size pieces. Chop the garlic and ginger and prepare the doubanjiang, stock, soy sauce, dried chilies, Sichuan peppercorns, and scallions.","Heat a wide wok and cook the cabbage for 1–2 minutes, until just tender but not limp. Transfer it to the bottom of a deep serving bowl.","Add a little oil to the wok if needed, then fry the doubanjiang, garlic, and ginger for 30–60 seconds, until fragrant and the oil turns red.","Pour in the stock and soy sauce, bring to a simmer, and cook for about 5 minutes so the broth takes on the chile-bean flavor.","Lower the heat to a gentle simmer. Add the fish slices one by one so they do not clump and poach for 2–4 minutes, depending on thickness, until opaque and just cooked through.","Taste the broth and adjust the salt before transferring the fish and broth over the cabbage.","Scatter dried chilies, Sichuan peppercorns, and scallions over the surface.","Heat a small amount of oil until shimmering and carefully pour it over the chilies and peppercorns so they sizzle and release their aroma.","Serve immediately with rice, warning diners that the topping oil and broth are very hot."],"cn_007":["Wash and dry the cucumbers. Lightly crush them with the flat side of a cleaver or rolling pin so the flesh splits irregularly, then cut into bite-size pieces.","Mix the rice vinegar, soy sauce, sesame oil, and minced garlic until combined.","Toss the cucumber pieces thoroughly with the dressing so it reaches the cracked surfaces.","Let the salad rest for about 5 minutes so the cucumber absorbs some seasoning while staying crisp.","Add scallions, toss once more, and serve immediately or lightly chilled."],"cn_008":["Cut the pork belly into large cubes. Bring a saucepan of water to a boil, blanch the pork for about 3 minutes, then drain and rinse away any foam.","Pat the pork dry. In a heavy pot, melt the brown sugar gently until it begins to dissolve and darken slightly, watching carefully so it does not burn.","Add the pork and turn the pieces through the caramelized sugar until lightly coated.","Add the light and dark soy sauces, Shaoxing wine, ginger, scallions, star anise, and cinnamon. Add enough water to come most of the way up the pork.","Bring to a boil, then lower to a very gentle simmer. Cover and braise for about 45 minutes, turning the pork occasionally.","Remove the lid and continue simmering for another 20–30 minutes, until the pork is very tender and the liquid has reduced substantially.","Turn the pieces gently as the sauce thickens so they glaze evenly without breaking apart.","When the sauce is glossy and clings to the pork, remove the whole spices and skim excess surface fat if desired.","Rest for 5 minutes and serve hot with rice."],"cn_009":["Cut the scallions into long sections and dry them well so they do not spit excessively in the oil.","Add the scallions and neutral oil to a skillet while it is still cool. Set over medium-low heat and cook slowly for 10–12 minutes, turning occasionally, until the scallions are deeply wilted, fragrant, and browned at the edges.","Lower the heat and stir in the light soy sauce, dark soy sauce, and sugar. Cook briefly until the sugar dissolves and the sauce is glossy, then remove from the heat.","Bring a pot of water to a boil and cook the wheat noodles for 3–5 minutes, or according to thickness, until just tender.","Reserve a little noodle water, then drain the noodles thoroughly.","Toss the hot noodles with enough scallion oil sauce to coat every strand; add a spoonful of noodle water only if needed to help the sauce spread.","Top with the cooked scallions and serve immediately."],"cn_010":["Finely chop the water chestnuts, scallions, and ginger. Combine them with the ground pork, soy sauce, Shaoxing wine, egg, and breadcrumbs.","Mix the pork mixture firmly in one direction for 2–3 minutes, until tacky and cohesive; this helps the large meatballs hold together.","Divide into 6 portions and shape into large round meatballs with damp hands.","Heat a deep skillet or wide pot over medium heat. Brown the meatballs gently for about 5–6 minutes total, turning carefully so several sides take on color without breaking.","Line the bottom of the pot with napa cabbage and place the browned meatballs on top.","Pour in enough light stock to come partway up the meatballs. Bring to a simmer, cover, and cook gently for 35–40 minutes.","Turn or baste the meatballs once or twice and check that the pork is fully cooked while the cabbage becomes very soft.","Taste the broth and adjust the seasoning lightly; the dish should remain delicate rather than heavily sauced.","Serve the meatballs with the braised cabbage and some of the broth."],"cn_011":["Combine the flour, instant yeast, and warm water and knead until a smooth, elastic dough forms. Cover and let it rest for 45–60 minutes, until slightly puffy rather than necessarily doubled.","While the dough rests, mix the ground pork, scallions, ginger, soy sauce, Shaoxing wine, and sesame oil in one direction until the filling becomes sticky and cohesive. Refrigerate the filling until needed.","Punch the dough down gently and divide it into 12 even pieces. Keep unused pieces covered so they do not dry out.","Roll each piece into a small round wrapper with the center slightly thicker than the edge.","Place a portion of chilled pork filling in the center, pleat the edge up around it, and pinch firmly closed. Set each bun seam-side up and keep covered while shaping the rest.","Heat a lightly oiled lidded skillet over medium heat. Arrange the buns with space between them and cook for 1–2 minutes, until the bottoms just begin to color.","Pour a measured shallow layer of water around the buns and cover immediately. Steam-fry for 8–10 minutes, until most of the water has evaporated and the pork filling is fully cooked.","Remove the lid carefully, allowing steam to escape away from you.","Continue cooking uncovered for 2–4 minutes, until the remaining moisture evaporates and the bases become deeply golden and crisp.","Scatter sesame seeds and scallions over the buns during the final minute if desired.","Rest the buns in the skillet for 1–2 minutes so the juices settle slightly.","Serve hot, warning that the filling can release very hot juices when bitten."],"cn_012":["Cut the pork shoulder into long strips of even thickness so the pieces roast at the same rate.","Mix the hoisin sauce, soy sauce, honey, Shaoxing wine, garlic, and five-spice powder until smooth.","Coat the pork thoroughly in the marinade, cover, and refrigerate for at least 6 hours or overnight, turning once if possible.","Remove the pork from the refrigerator while the oven heats. Arrange the strips on a rack over a lined roasting tray so hot air can circulate around them.","Roast for about 20 minutes, then turn the pieces and brush with a portion of clean reserved marinade or freshly mixed glaze.","Continue roasting for another 20–25 minutes, turning or glazing again if needed, until the pork is fully cooked and the surface is deeply lacquered.","If more color is needed, finish briefly under a hot broiler while watching constantly because the honey can burn quickly.","Rest the char siu for 10 minutes before slicing across the grain and serving."],"cn_013":["Bring a saucepan of water to a boil and cook the small tapioca pearls according to their size until mostly translucent, stirring so they do not stick together.","Turn off the heat, cover, and let the pearls stand for a few minutes if their centers are still opaque, then rinse thoroughly under cold water and drain.","Separate the pomelo flesh from the thick membranes so only juicy individual vesicles or small clusters remain. Dice the mango and reserve some neat pieces for garnish.","Blend the remaining mango with coconut milk, evaporated milk, and sugar until very smooth.","Stir the cooled tapioca pearls into the mango mixture and refrigerate for at least 30 minutes so the dessert becomes thoroughly chilled.","Taste after chilling and adjust the sweetness only if needed, since ripe mango contributes significant sugar.","Divide among bowls and top with pomelo and the reserved mango immediately before serving."],"cn_014":["Pat the prepared whole fish dry and make shallow diagonal cuts through the thickest flesh so it cooks evenly. Place it on a heatproof steaming plate with ginger.","Bring the steamer water to a strong boil before placing the fish inside.","Steam for 8–12 minutes, depending on the thickness of the fish, without repeatedly opening the lid.","Check the thickest part near the backbone; the flesh should be opaque, moist, and flake cleanly when fully cooked.","Carefully pour off excess steaming liquid from the plate if it is watery, then scatter fresh scallion and ginger over the fish.","Warm the light soy sauce and optional Shaoxing wine briefly and spoon it around and over the fish.","Serve immediately while the fish is hot and delicate."],"cn_015":["Slice the flank steak thinly across the grain. Toss with soy sauce, Shaoxing wine, and cornstarch and let it marinate for 15 minutes.","Gently separate the wide rice noodles with your hands. Slice the onion and scallions and rinse and dry the bean sprouts; have everything beside the wok before cooking.","Heat a large wok over high heat until very hot. Stir-fry the beef in a single layer for 1–2 minutes per side, until browned but not quite fully cooked, then remove it.","Add the onion to the wok and stir-fry briefly until it begins to soften.","Add the rice noodles and spread them across the hot wok. Let them sear in places before turning gently so they develop wok char without tearing.","Drizzle in the soy seasoning around the side of the wok and toss until the noodles are evenly colored.","Return the beef and add the scallions and bean sprouts. Toss over high heat for 1–2 minutes, until the beef is fully cooked and the sprouts are just tender.","Keep the wok dry enough that the noodles fry rather than simmer; if liquid pools, increase the heat and toss briefly to evaporate it.","Serve immediately."],"cn_016":["Rinse the jasmine rice until the water is mostly clear, then soak it for about 20 minutes and drain well.","Slice the Chinese sausage and shiitake mushrooms. Cut the chicken thigh into bite-size pieces and season it with soy sauce, oyster sauce, and Shaoxing wine.","Put the drained rice in the clay pot with the measured cooking water. Bring to a boil, then cover and cook over low heat for about 10 minutes.","When the rice has absorbed much of the surface water but is still undercooked, quickly arrange the sausage, chicken, and mushrooms over the top.","Cover again and cook over low heat for 12–15 minutes, until the rice is tender and the chicken is fully cooked.","During the final few minutes, raise the heat slightly for short intervals so a crisp rice crust forms on the bottom, listening for gentle crackling but stopping if you smell scorching.","Blanch the choy sum separately until bright green and tender-crisp, then drain.","Turn off the heat and let the covered pot rest for 5 minutes so the rice finishes steaming.","Add the blanched greens and spoon over a little extra soy-oyster seasoning if desired.","Mix from the bottom at the table so pieces of the crisp crust are distributed through the rice."],"cn_017":["Chop part of the shrimp finely and leave some in small pieces for texture. Mix with the ground pork, soy sauce, sesame oil, Shaoxing wine, and scallions until the filling becomes sticky.","Cover and chill the filling for at least 15 minutes while you set up the wrappers, noodle pot, serving broth, and choy sum.","Place a small spoonful of filling in each wonton wrapper, moisten the edges, fold, and press firmly to seal. Keep finished wontons covered so the wrappers do not dry out.","Bring a large pot of water to a gentle boil. Cook the wontons in batches for 4–6 minutes, until the wrappers are tender and the pork-and-shrimp filling is fully cooked.","Lift the wontons out with a slotted spoon and keep them warm without crowding them together.","Cook the egg noodles separately according to thickness until springy and tender, then drain well.","Blanch the choy sum briefly in the noodle water and drain.","Bring the light stock to a simmer and season it gently with soy sauce; keep the broth clean and not overly salty.","Divide noodles among bowls, add wontons and choy sum, and ladle over the boiling-hot broth.","Finish with scallions and serve immediately."],"cn_018":["Chop half of the shrimp into a paste and cut the rest into small chunks. Mix with finely chopped bamboo shoots, soy sauce, sesame oil, and cornstarch until tacky, then refrigerate while making the wrappers.","Combine the wheat starch and tapioca starch in a heatproof bowl.","Pour in boiling water gradually while stirring with chopsticks or a sturdy spoon until a shaggy dough forms; cover and let it cool just enough to handle safely.","Knead the warm dough until smooth and pliable. Keep it covered at all times because starch dough dries quickly.","Divide the dough into small equal pieces. Work with one at a time and flatten it into a thin round wrapper, keeping the center slightly thicker.","Place a modest amount of chilled shrimp filling in the center and pleat the wrapper closed without overfilling.","Line a steamer basket and arrange the dumplings with space between them.","Bring the steamer water to a full boil before adding the basket.","Steam the har gow for 5–6 minutes, until the wrappers turn translucent and the shrimp filling is fully cooked.","Remove the basket from the heat and let the dumplings stand for 1–2 minutes so the delicate wrappers firm slightly.","Serve immediately while the wrappers are soft and translucent."],"cn_019":["Finely chop the shrimp and rehydrated shiitake mushrooms. Combine with the ground pork, scallions, soy sauce, oyster sauce, Shaoxing wine, and sesame oil.","Mix the filling vigorously in one direction for 2–3 minutes, until tacky and cohesive, then chill it for about 15 minutes.","Place a wrapper in your hand, add a spoonful of filling, and gather the wrapper around it to form an open-topped cylinder. Flatten the base gently so the dumpling stands upright.","Repeat with the remaining wrappers, keeping finished siu mai covered so they do not dry out.","Line a steamer and arrange the dumplings with a little space between them.","Bring the steamer water to a strong boil, then steam the siu mai for 8–10 minutes.","Check that the pork is fully cooked and the shrimp is opaque before removing the basket from the heat.","Rest for 1–2 minutes and serve hot."],"cn_020":["Rehydrate the shiitake mushrooms if dried, then dice them and the Chinese sausage finely. Cook both briefly in a skillet until fragrant and some fat renders from the sausage; set aside.","Peel and grate the daikon. Put it in a wide pan and cook for 8–10 minutes, stirring, until softened and much of its raw moisture has released.","Mix the rice flour and cornstarch with enough cool water to make a smooth, lump-free slurry.","Stir the sausage, mushrooms, scallions, and white pepper into the cooked daikon.","Lower the heat and gradually stir in the flour slurry. Cook just until the mixture becomes very thick and cohesive, scraping the bottom so it does not scorch.","Pack the mixture into a greased heatproof pan, smoothing the top and pressing out large air pockets.","Steam over steadily boiling water for 50–60 minutes, or until the center is fully set and a skewer comes out without wet batter.","Cool completely, then refrigerate until firm enough to slice cleanly; chilling makes frying much easier.","Cut the chilled cake into thick slices and pan-fry over medium heat until golden and crisp on both sides.","Serve hot with your preferred dipping sauce."],"cn_021":["Rinse the rice until the water is mostly clear and drain well.","Combine the rice and chicken stock in a heavy saucepan and bring to a boil, stirring once to prevent grains from sticking to the bottom.","Reduce to a very gentle simmer and cook for 40–45 minutes, stirring the bottom every several minutes as the porridge thickens.","Add the ginger during the final 10 minutes so its flavor stays fresh but has time to infuse the congee.","Stir in the cooked shredded chicken and simmer for about 5 minutes, until the chicken is piping hot.","Adjust the consistency with additional hot stock if the congee is thicker than you like, then taste and season lightly.","Ladle into bowls and finish with scallions, sesame oil, and white pepper."],"cn_022":["Prepare the cucumber, carrot, bean sprouts, and scallions and keep them separate so they stay crisp for serving.","Heat a wok over medium-high heat and cook the ground pork for 5–6 minutes, breaking it into small pieces, until fully cooked and lightly browned.","Add the sweet bean sauce and yellow bean paste and fry them with the pork for 1–2 minutes, stirring constantly so they caramelize without burning.","Pour in the Shaoxing wine and a small amount of water, scraping up any browned bits from the wok.","Lower the heat and simmer the pork-bean sauce for 15–18 minutes, stirring occasionally, until thick, glossy, and intensely savory.","Meanwhile, boil the wheat noodles until tender but springy, then drain well.","Divide the noodles among bowls and spoon the hot zhajiang over the top.","Arrange cucumber, carrot, bean sprouts, and scallions over each bowl and mix thoroughly before eating."],"cn_023":["Whisk the all-purpose flour, mung bean flour, and water into a smooth, pourable batter. Cover and let it rest for about 15 minutes.","Prepare the eggs, sliced scallions, sesame seeds, crisp wonton sheets, sweet bean sauce, and optional chile sauce beside the stove.","Heat a large flat nonstick skillet or griddle over medium heat and lightly grease it.","Pour in a thin layer of batter and spread it quickly into a wide crepe. Cook until the surface is mostly set and the underside releases easily.","Crack an egg over the crepe and spread it into a thin layer across the surface. Sprinkle with scallions and sesame seeds.","When the egg is nearly set, flip the crepe carefully and cook the second side briefly.","Spread sweet bean sauce and optional chile sauce over the cooked side.","Place a crisp wonton sheet in the center, fold the crepe around it, and press lightly so the package holds together.","Serve immediately while the crepe is hot and the center remains crisp."],"cn_024":["Finely chop the napa cabbage, toss it with salt, and let it stand for about 10 minutes. Squeeze very thoroughly to remove excess water.","Combine the ground pork, squeezed cabbage, scallions, ginger, soy sauce, and sesame oil and mix until cohesive and slightly tacky.","Cook a teaspoon of filling in a small pan and taste it so you can correct the seasoning before wrapping the full batch.","Place a small portion of filling in the center of each dumpling wrapper. Moisten the edge, fold, and pleat or press firmly to seal.","Keep finished jiaozi covered with a damp towel so the wrappers do not dry out.","Bring a large pot of water to a steady but not violent boil and add the dumplings in batches without overcrowding.","Stir gently once so they do not stick to the bottom, then cook for about 5–7 minutes, until the wrappers are tender and the pork filling is fully cooked.","Lift the dumplings out with a slotted spoon and drain briefly.","Serve hot with Chinkiang vinegar."],"cn_025":["Mix the flour and warm water into a rough dough, then knead until smooth. Cover and let it rest for about 30 minutes so the gluten relaxes.","Finely slice the scallions and have salt and cooking oil ready for layering.","Divide the rested dough into equal portions and keep unused pieces covered.","Roll one portion into a very thin rectangle or oval. Brush lightly with oil and sprinkle evenly with salt and scallions.","Roll the sheet into a tight rope, then coil the rope into a spiral. Tuck the end underneath so the coil holds its shape.","Repeat with the remaining dough and let the coils rest for another 10 minutes.","Gently roll each rested coil into a flat pancake, taking care not to force out all the internal layers.","Heat a lightly oiled skillet over medium heat. Cook each pancake for several minutes per side, turning as needed, until both sides are golden.","Lower the heat if necessary and continue cooking until the center is cooked through while the outside stays crisp.","Rest briefly, cut into wedges, and serve warm."],"cn_026":["Mix the flour, water, and salt into a firm dough and knead until smooth. Cover and let it rest for 45–60 minutes so it becomes extensible enough to stretch without tearing.","Divide the dough into even pieces, shape them into short logs, lightly coat with oil, and rest covered for another 15–20 minutes if the dough still feels tight.","Prepare the serving bowls with soy sauce and Chinkiang vinegar. Mince the garlic, slice the scallions, and prepare the spiced seasoning and optional bok choy.","Bring a large pot of water to a vigorous boil.","Flatten one dough piece into a strip, press a groove down the center, then slap and stretch it repeatedly until it forms a very long, wide noodle. Tear along the center groove if making the classic belt-like shape.","Drop the stretched noodles directly into boiling water and repeat with the remaining pieces in manageable batches.","Cook for about 2–3 minutes, until the noodles are chewy and tender with no raw floury center. Blanch the bok choy in the same water if using.","Drain the noodles well and divide them among the seasoned bowls.","Pile the minced garlic, scallions, and spiced seasoning over the hot noodles.","Heat a small amount of oil until shimmering and carefully pour it over the aromatics so they sizzle intensely.","Mix thoroughly from the bottom and serve immediately."],"cn_027":["Cut the pork shoulder into large chunks. Add it to a heavy pot with soy sauce, Shaoxing wine, brown sugar, ginger, star anise, cinnamon, and enough water to come most of the way up the meat.","Bring to a boil, skim any foam, then lower to a gentle simmer and cover partially.","Braise for 75–90 minutes, turning the pork occasionally, until it is very tender and easily pulled apart.","While the pork braises, combine the flour, water, and yeast and knead into a smooth dough. Cover and let it rise for about 45 minutes.","Divide the dough into small equal portions. Roll each into a short oval, fold or coil according to your preferred baijimo shape, then flatten into thick rounds.","Cook the flatbreads in a dry or very lightly oiled skillet over medium-low heat, turning several times, until both sides are browned and the centers are fully cooked.","Remove the tender pork from the braising liquid and chop it finely on a board.","Simmer the braising liquid uncovered until slightly reduced and glossy, then spoon just enough into the chopped pork to make it juicy without becoming soupy.","Taste the chopped meat and adjust the salt only after reduction.","Split each warm flatbread almost all the way through, leaving one edge attached like a hinge.","Pack generously with the chopped braised pork.","Serve immediately while the bread is warm and the filling is juicy."],"cn_028":["Rinse the rice until the water is mostly clear, then soak for about 20 minutes and drain thoroughly.","Cut the carrots into thin batons and slice the onion.","Heat a heavy pot over medium heat and cook the onion until softened and lightly golden, then add the carrots and cook until they begin to soften.","Stir in the cumin and raisins so the spices coat the vegetables.","Pour in the stock and season with salt, then spread the drained rice evenly over the vegetables without stirring it deeply into the lower layer.","Bring to a boil, cover tightly, lower the heat, and cook for about 25 minutes, until the rice has absorbed the liquid and is tender.","Turn off the heat and let the covered pilaf rest for 10 minutes so the grains finish steaming.","Fluff from the bottom, mixing the carrots, onions, and raisins gently through the rice before serving."],"cn_029":["Separate the bok choy stems if very large, rinse thoroughly, and drain until the leaves are no longer dripping wet.","Heat a wok over high heat and add the garlic. Stir for only 15–30 seconds, until fragrant but not browned.","Add the bok choy and stir-fry vigorously for 3–5 minutes, turning the thicker stems against the hot wok, until the vegetables are bright green and tender-crisp.","Drizzle in the soy sauce around the side of the wok and toss for another 20–30 seconds.","Serve immediately so the bok choy stays crisp and vivid."],"cn_030":["Tear the cabbage by hand into irregular bite-size pieces, discarding the toughest core. Rinse and dry thoroughly.","Prepare the garlic, vinegar, and soy sauce before heating the wok.","Heat the wok over high heat and add the garlic, stirring for only 15–30 seconds until fragrant.","Add the cabbage and stir-fry for 4–6 minutes, tossing constantly, until the edges soften and blister while the thicker pieces retain some bite.","Drizzle the vinegar and soy sauce around the hot side of the wok so they sizzle before being tossed through the cabbage.","Taste, adjust the seasoning, and serve immediately."],"cn_031":["Cut the eggplant into long, even pieces and arrange them in a single layer or loose stack on a heatproof plate.","Bring the steamer water to a full boil before placing the plate inside.","Steam the eggplant for 10–12 minutes, until completely tender when pierced but still holding its shape.","While it steams, mix the chopped pickled chili, minced garlic, and soy sauce.","Spoon the chile-garlic mixture over the steaming-hot eggplant so the heat releases the aromatics.","Finish with scallions and serve warm or at room temperature."],"cn_032":["Mix the peanut butter, sesame oil, soy sauce, and rice vinegar until smooth and glossy.","Bring a large saucepan of water to a boil and cook the wheat noodles for about 3–5 minutes, or according to thickness, until tender with a little bite.","Before draining, reserve about a cup of hot noodle water.","Whisk a little reserved noodle water into the peanut dressing a spoonful at a time until it becomes creamy and pourable rather than stiff.","Drain the noodles well and toss them immediately with the loosened peanut sauce until every strand is coated.","Finish with scallions and serve hot or warm."],"cn_033":["Prepare all ingredients before beginning: chop the pickled mustard greens and tomato, mince the garlic, and slice the scallions and cilantro.","Heat a small saucepan or wok over medium-high heat and cook the ground pork for 4–5 minutes, breaking it into small crumbles, until fully cooked.","Add the tomato, garlic, and pickled mustard greens and cook for 1–2 minutes, until the tomato begins to soften and the aromatics smell fragrant.","Pour in the light stock and soy sauce and bring to a gentle simmer.","Simmer for about 5 minutes so the broth picks up flavor from the pork, tomato, and pickled greens.","Add the fresh rice noodles and cook in the broth for 2–4 minutes, depending on thickness, until tender without becoming mushy.","Taste and adjust the broth, remembering the pickled greens may already contribute significant salt.","Pour into bowls and finish with scallions and cilantro."],"cn_034":["Cut the chicken into medium serving pieces and pat them dry. Slice the ginger and scallions and clean the shiitake mushrooms.","Arrange the chicken, ginger, scallions, and mushrooms in the steam pot or a deep heatproof bowl, leaving some space for steam circulation.","Set the vessel over a pot of steadily boiling water and cover it tightly so condensed steam collects inside rather than escaping.","Steam for about 90 minutes, checking the external water level periodically and replenishing it with hot water as needed.","As the chicken cooks, its juices and condensed steam should form a clear broth without adding a large amount of water directly to the vessel.","Continue until every chicken piece is fully cooked and tender, extending the steaming time if necessary for larger pieces.","Skim excess surface fat if desired, season the clear broth with salt and white pepper, and serve the chicken in its broth."],"cn_035":["Bring the light stock to a gentle simmer and season it with soy sauce. Let it simmer for about 15 minutes so the serving broth tastes savory but remains light.","Slice the pork or beef thinly across the grain and prepare the pickled green beans, roasted peanuts, scallions, and cilantro.","Add the sliced meat to the simmering broth and cook only until fully cooked and tender, then keep it warm in a small amount of broth.","Bring a separate pot of water to a boil and cook the rice noodles until tender but not mushy.","Drain the noodles very well and divide them among serving bowls.","Arrange the cooked meat over the noodles and add a modest amount of hot broth rather than turning the dish into a deep soup.","Top each bowl with pickled green beans, roasted peanuts, scallions, and cilantro.","Serve immediately and let diners mix the contrasting toppings through the noodles."],"cn_036":["Trim the snow peas, remove any tough strings, wash them, and drain very well.","Heat a wok over high heat and stir the garlic for 15–30 seconds, until fragrant but not browned.","Add the snow peas and stir-fry for 3–4 minutes, tossing continuously, until bright green and tender-crisp.","Season with salt and a small amount of soy sauce and toss for another 20–30 seconds.","Serve immediately so the peas remain crisp."],"cn_037":["Break up the cold cooked rice so the grains are separate. Dice the carrot, slice the scallions, and prepare the cooked shrimp and char siu or ham before heating the wok.","Heat the wok over high heat, add the beaten eggs, and scramble for 30–45 seconds, until softly set. Transfer the eggs to a plate.","Add the shrimp, char siu or ham, peas, and carrot and stir-fry briefly until everything is hot; if the shrimp is not already fully cooked, cook it until opaque before adding the rice.","Add the cold rice and toss over high heat for 4–5 minutes, breaking up any remaining clumps and allowing some grains to toast against the wok.","Drizzle in a modest amount of soy sauce around the hot side of the wok and toss until the seasoning is even without turning the rice wet or dark.","Return the eggs and toss gently so the curds remain visible rather than disappearing into the rice.","Add the scallions and white pepper for the final 30 seconds.","Serve immediately while the grains are hot and separate."],"cn_038":["Mix the glutinous rice flour with warm water a little at a time until a soft, smooth dough forms that does not crack when pressed.","Divide the black sesame paste into small portions and chill briefly if it is too soft to handle.","Pinch off a piece of dough, roll it into a ball, and flatten it into a small cup in your palm.","Place one portion of sesame filling in the center, bring the dough up around it, pinch closed, and roll gently into a smooth ball.","Keep shaped tangyuan covered while you finish the batch so the surfaces do not dry out.","Bring a saucepan of water to a gentle boil and add the dumplings without overcrowding.","Cook until they float, then continue simmering for another 1–2 minutes so the glutinous dough is cooked through to the center.","Serve warm with a little of the cooking liquid or in a light sweet soup."],"cn_039":["Combine the milk, water, sugar, and agar powder in a saucepan and whisk thoroughly before heating so the agar is evenly dispersed.","Bring to a gentle boil while stirring, then simmer briefly until the agar is completely dissolved; undissolved agar can prevent a clean set.","Remove from the heat and stir in the almond extract.","Pour the mixture into a shallow dish and let it cool until no longer steaming strongly.","Refrigerate for about 2 hours, or until completely firm and chilled.","Cut into cubes and serve cold with fresh fruit if desired."],"cn_040":["Rinse the dried smoked plums, dried hawthorn, and licorice root quickly under cool water to remove surface dust.","Place the dried ingredients in a stockpot with the measured water and let them soak for about 30 minutes.","Bring the pot to a boil, then reduce to a gentle simmer and cook for about 30 minutes.","Turn off the heat and stir in the rock sugar until completely dissolved, tasting before adding more because the fruit is naturally tart.","Strain the drink through a fine sieve and discard the solids.","Cool completely at room temperature, then refrigerate for at least 1 hour.","Serve well chilled, over ice if desired."],"th_001":["Soak the flat rice noodles in room-temperature water for 25–30 minutes, or until flexible but still firm in the center. Drain very well so excess water does not dilute the sauce.","Stir the tamarind concentrate, fish sauce, and palm sugar together until the sugar is mostly dissolved. Taste the sauce; it should be distinctly sweet, sour, and salty because the noodles will soften the seasoning.","Cut the tofu into small pieces and prepare the protein, eggs, bean sprouts, scallions, peanuts, and lime before heating the wok; Pad Thai cooks quickly once it starts.","Heat the wok over medium-high to high heat. Add the shrimp or chicken and tofu and stir-fry for about 3–4 minutes, until the protein is almost cooked through.","Push the protein to one side, crack in the eggs, and let them set for a few seconds before scrambling into soft curds, about 45–60 seconds.","Add the drained noodles and prepared sauce. Toss and lift the noodles continuously for 2–4 minutes, adding a small splash of water only if they remain too firm, until the noodles are tender but still pleasantly chewy and the sauce has been absorbed.","Return everything to the center of the wok and check that the shrimp is opaque or the chicken is fully cooked before proceeding.","Add most of the bean sprouts and scallions and toss for only 30–60 seconds so they remain crisp.","Transfer immediately to plates and finish with roasted peanuts, the remaining fresh sprouts if desired, and lime wedges. Serve at once before the noodles soften."],"th_002":["Pound the garlic and Thai chilies together into a coarse paste. Mix the oyster sauce, fish sauce, light soy sauce, dark soy sauce, and sugar in a small bowl so the seasoning is ready before the wok gets hot.","Pick the holy basil leaves from the stems, rinse if needed, and dry them thoroughly.","Heat a wok over high heat until very hot. Add the ground pork or chicken and stir-fry, breaking it into small pieces, for about 4–5 minutes.","When the meat is nearly cooked, move it aside briefly and add the garlic-chile paste to the exposed hot surface. Stir for about 20–30 seconds until sharply fragrant without burning the garlic.","Toss the aromatics through the meat and continue cooking until the meat is fully cooked and any excess liquid has mostly evaporated.","Pour in the prepared sauce and stir-fry for another 1–2 minutes, until the seasoning coats the meat rather than pooling in the wok.","Turn off the heat or reduce it to very low, add the holy basil, and toss just until the leaves wilt and release their aroma.","Serve immediately over hot jasmine rice; if adding a fried egg, cook it separately and place it on top."],"th_003":["Trim the lemongrass, bruise the stalks, tear the makrut lime leaves, slice the galangal, clean the mushrooms, and peel and devein the shrimp while keeping the shrimp chilled.","Bring the stock to a gentle boil. Add lemongrass, galangal, and makrut lime leaves and simmer for about 8 minutes so the broth becomes aromatic.","Add the mushrooms and simmer 3–4 minutes, until just tender.","Add the shrimp and cook only 2–3 minutes, depending on size, until pink, opaque, and just firm. Do not keep boiling once the shrimp is done.","Stir in the fish sauce and sugar while the soup is still hot, then remove the pot from the heat.","Add the lime juice and crushed or sliced Thai chilies off the heat so the lime stays bright rather than becoming bitter.","Taste and adjust with a little more fish sauce, lime, or sugar as needed; the soup should be hot, sour, salty, and aromatic rather than sweet.","Ladle into bowls, leaving the tough aromatics in the broth for fragrance but not necessarily eating them, and finish with cilantro."],"th_004":["Bruise the lemongrass, slice the galangal, tear the makrut lime leaves, cut the chicken into bite-size pieces, and clean and slice the mushrooms.","Combine the stock, half of the coconut milk, lemongrass, galangal, and lime leaves in a saucepan. Bring to a gentle simmer and cook for about 8 minutes.","Add the chicken and keep the liquid at a quiet simmer rather than a hard boil. Poach for about 7–9 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the chicken is nearly cooked through.","Add the mushrooms and simmer another 3–4 minutes, until tender and the chicken is fully cooked.","Pour in the remaining coconut milk and add the fish sauce and palm sugar. Heat gently for 1–2 minutes without letting the coconut milk boil violently or split.","Remove the pot from the heat before stirring in the lime juice and Thai chilies.","Taste and adjust the balance: the broth should be creamy, salty, tangy, lightly sweet, and strongly scented with galangal and lime.","Serve hot, leaving the fibrous lemongrass, galangal, and lime leaves in the bowl for aroma but not necessarily eating them."],"th_005":["Shake or stir the coconut milk so you can use the thickest cream first. Cut the chicken, Thai eggplant, and bamboo shoots into serving pieces and tear the makrut lime leaves.","Spoon the thick coconut cream into a saucepan over medium heat and cook for 2–3 minutes, stirring, until glossy and beginning to release a little oil.","Add the green curry paste and fry it in the coconut cream for about 2 minutes, until the raw paste smell is gone and the mixture is very fragrant.","Add the chicken and stir for 3–4 minutes so every piece is coated in curry paste and the outside begins to cook.","Pour in the remaining coconut milk and stock. Add the makrut lime leaves and bring to a gentle simmer.","Add the Thai eggplant and bamboo shoots and simmer for about 10–12 minutes, until the eggplant is tender and the chicken is fully cooked.","Season with fish sauce and palm sugar, adding each gradually until the curry is savory, slightly sweet, and aromatic rather than sugary.","If the curry is too thick, loosen it with a little stock; if too thin, simmer uncovered for a few minutes rather than adding starch.","Turn off the heat, fold in the Thai basil, and let it wilt for about 30 seconds.","Serve immediately with jasmine rice while the basil and curry aromatics are fresh."],"th_006":["Cut the chicken, bamboo shoots, and bell pepper into even pieces and tear the makrut lime leaves. Keep the Thai basil leaves separate for the finish.","Cook the thickest coconut cream over medium heat until glossy and beginning to release a little oil.","Add the red curry paste and fry for about 2 minutes, stirring constantly, until fragrant and slightly darkened.","Add the chicken and stir for 3–4 minutes so the pieces are thoroughly coated and beginning to cook.","Add the remaining coconut milk, stock, bamboo shoots, and makrut lime leaves. Bring to a gentle simmer.","Simmer for about 8–10 minutes, then add the bell pepper and cook another 4–5 minutes, until the pepper is just tender and the chicken is fully cooked.","Season gradually with fish sauce and palm sugar, tasting for a rounded salty-sweet-spicy balance.","Simmer uncovered for a few minutes if the curry needs to thicken, or loosen with a little stock if it becomes too concentrated.","Turn off the heat and fold in the Thai basil just until wilted.","Serve hot with jasmine rice."],"th_007":["Cut the beef chuck into large, even cubes. Cut the potatoes and onion into large pieces so they can withstand a long simmer.","Cook the thick coconut cream in a heavy pot over medium heat for 2–3 minutes, until glossy. Add the massaman curry paste and fry another 2–3 minutes until very fragrant.","Add the beef and stir for 4–5 minutes so the cubes are coated and lightly colored on the outside.","Pour in the remaining coconut milk and beef stock and add the cinnamon stick. Bring just to a simmer.","Cover partially and simmer very gently for 70–80 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the beef is becoming tender but is not yet falling apart.","Add the potatoes and onion. Continue simmering for another 25–35 minutes, until the potatoes are tender and the beef yields easily to a fork.","Stir in the fish sauce, palm sugar, and tamarind concentrate. Taste and adjust gradually; massaman should be savory, gently sweet, tangy, and warmly spiced.","Add the roasted peanuts and simmer uncovered for 5–10 minutes so the sauce thickens enough to coat the beef and potatoes.","Remove the cinnamon stick, skim excess surface fat if desired, and let the curry rest for 5–10 minutes before serving.","Serve with jasmine rice."],"th_008":["Thinly slice the pork shoulder across the grain and finely shred the makrut lime leaves. Pick the Thai basil leaves.","Cook the thick coconut cream over medium heat until glossy and beginning to release a little oil.","Add the panang curry paste and fry for about 2 minutes, stirring constantly, until aromatic.","Add the pork and stir-fry for 4–5 minutes so the slices are coated and nearly cooked.","Pour in the remaining coconut milk and bring to a gentle simmer. Cook 8–10 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the pork is fully cooked and the sauce has thickened.","Season with fish sauce and palm sugar a little at a time; panang should be rich and concentrated, not soupy.","Stir in most of the finely sliced makrut lime leaves and simmer 1 minute.","Remove from the heat and fold in the Thai basil just until wilted.","Serve with jasmine rice and the remaining lime-leaf shreds on top."],"th_009":["Break up the cold jasmine rice with your fingers or a rice paddle so there are no hard clumps. Prepare the protein, eggs, onion, garlic, scallions, and seasonings before heating the wok.","Heat a wok over high heat. Add the chicken or shrimp and stir-fry for about 3–4 minutes, until nearly cooked; move it to one side or briefly remove it.","Add the eggs to the open side of the wok and scramble for 30–45 seconds, until softly set.","Add the onion and garlic and stir-fry about 1 minute, until fragrant but not browned heavily.","Add the cold rice, spreading it across the hot wok. Toss over high heat for 3–4 minutes until the grains are hot, separate, and lightly toasted in spots.","Drizzle in the fish sauce and light soy sauce around the hot side of the wok, sprinkle in the sugar, and toss until the seasoning is evenly distributed.","Return or redistribute the protein and continue tossing until chicken is fully cooked or shrimp is opaque and the rice is steaming hot.","Fold in the scallions for the final 30 seconds.","Serve immediately with lime wedges."],"th_010":["Gently separate the fresh wide rice noodles with lightly oiled fingers so they do not tear. Slice the protein and separate the Chinese broccoli stems from the leaves.","Mix the dark soy sauce, light soy sauce, oyster sauce, and sugar in a small bowl.","Heat a wok over high heat. Stir-fry the chicken, pork, or beef for about 3–4 minutes, until nearly cooked, then push it to the side.","Crack in the eggs and scramble them into soft curds, about 45–60 seconds.","Add the Chinese broccoli stems and stir-fry for about 1 minute, then add the leaves for another 30–60 seconds.","Add the noodles and sauce. Toss gently but decisively for 2–3 minutes, allowing parts of the noodles to sit against the hot wok long enough to take on light char without breaking.","Check that the protein is fully cooked and the noodles are hot and tender but not mushy.","Serve immediately."],"th_011":["Gently separate the wide rice noodles and cut the Chinese broccoli, keeping thicker stems separate from leaves. Slice the pork or chicken evenly.","Mix the oyster sauce, soy sauce, and sugar into the stock. Keep the tapioca starch slurry separate and stir it again just before using.","Heat a wok until very hot. Stir-fry the noodles for 2–3 minutes, turning carefully, until hot and lightly charred in spots. Divide among serving plates.","Return the wok to the heat and cook the protein for 3–4 minutes, until nearly done.","Pour in the seasoned stock and simmer for about 3 minutes.","Add the Chinese broccoli stems first, then the leaves, and simmer 2–3 minutes until tender-crisp and the protein is fully cooked.","Stir the starch slurry, drizzle it into the simmering liquid while stirring, and cook 1–2 minutes until the gravy is glossy and thick enough to coat a spoon.","Taste and adjust the seasoning, then spoon the hot gravy generously over the charred noodles and serve immediately."],"th_012":["Crush or bruise the garlic and coriander roots and gather the cinnamon and star anise. Slice the beef or pork very thinly and keep it chilled until the broth is ready.","Bring the stock to a simmer with the garlic, coriander roots, cinnamon, and star anise. Simmer gently for about 40 minutes so the broth develops a deep aromatic flavor.","Strain out spent aromatics if you want a cleaner broth, then season with soy sauce, fish sauce, dark soy sauce, and brown sugar. Simmer another 5 minutes and taste; boat-noodle broth should be intensely savory.","Prepare the bean sprouts, morning glory, and Thai basil while the broth stays hot.","Cook the rice noodles separately according to thickness until just tender, then drain well.","Blanch the bean sprouts and morning glory briefly, about 30–60 seconds, so they soften without losing all their crunch.","Add the thinly sliced meat to the simmering broth in small batches and cook 2–4 minutes, or until fully cooked without becoming tough.","Divide noodles and vegetables among bowls, arrange the cooked meat over them, and ladle in enough boiling-hot broth to heat everything through.","Finish with Thai basil and serve immediately."],"th_013":["Rinse the chicken and remove loose excess fat; reserve a little chicken fat if available for the rice. Bruise the ginger and coriander roots and prepare a pot large enough to hold the chicken.","Place the chicken in water with ginger and coriander roots and bring slowly to a bare simmer. Keep the liquid below a hard boil so the meat stays tender.","Poach for about 35–45 minutes, depending on whether using a whole chicken or pieces, until the thickest meat is fully cooked. Remove the chicken and rest it while keeping it covered.","Strain the poaching broth. Reserve enough measured broth to cook the jasmine rice and keep the remainder hot to serve as soup.","Rinse the rice and drain well. Cook minced garlic in a little rendered chicken fat or oil until fragrant, then stir in the rice for 1–2 minutes so the grains are coated.","Add the measured chicken broth and cook the rice covered until tender and fragrant. Rest covered for 10 minutes before fluffing.","For the dipping sauce, stir soybean paste, soy sauce, vinegar, sugar, ginger, and chopped Thai chilies until the sugar dissolves. Adjust so it tastes salty, tangy, and slightly sweet.","Debone or chop the rested chicken into serving pieces and slice the cucumber.","Serve the chicken over the fragrant rice with cucumber and dipping sauce.","Ladle the clear hot chicken broth into small bowls and serve alongside."],"th_014":["Pound the garlic, coriander roots, and white pepper into a smooth aromatic paste.","Mix the paste with coconut milk, fish sauce, light soy sauce, and palm sugar until the sugar dissolves.","Toss the thin pork shoulder slices thoroughly in the marinade. Cover and refrigerate for at least 3 hours, or longer for deeper flavor.","Soak bamboo skewers in water if needed. Thread the pork onto the skewers in loose folds rather than packing it tightly, leaving a small handle.","Heat the grill to medium or medium-high. Let excess marinade drip from the skewers so the sugar does not burn immediately.","Grill for about 3–4 minutes per side, turning as needed, until caramelized at the edges and fully cooked through.","Brush with a little clean coconut milk during grilling if you want a glossier surface, but do not reuse raw-meat marinade unless it has been boiled.","Rest the skewers for 2–3 minutes and serve hot with sticky rice."],"th_015":["Prepare the chicken thighs and toppings first: chop the pickled mustard greens, slice the shallots, cut the lime, and have the crispy noodles ready for serving.","Cook the thick coconut cream in a heavy pot over medium heat until glossy.","Add the red curry paste, curry powder, and turmeric and fry for 2–3 minutes, stirring, until deeply fragrant and the paste loses its raw smell.","Add the chicken and stir for about 4 minutes so the pieces are thoroughly coated and begin to cook.","Pour in the remaining coconut milk and chicken stock. Bring to a gentle simmer, partially cover, and cook 30–35 minutes, until the chicken is tender and fully cooked.","Season with fish sauce and palm sugar, adding each gradually until the broth is rich, savory, lightly sweet, and aromatic.","While the curry simmers, boil the egg noodles according to their thickness until springy and tender, then drain well.","Divide the cooked noodles among bowls and place chicken over each portion.","Ladle the hot coconut curry broth over the noodles.","Finish each bowl with pickled mustard greens, sliced shallot, lime, and crispy noodles; serve immediately so the fried noodles stay crisp."],"th_016":["Very finely chop the lemongrass, makrut lime leaves, cilantro stems, and scallions so no large fibrous pieces remain in the sausage.","Mix the ground pork with red curry paste, turmeric, fish sauce, and the prepared herbs. Knead or stir vigorously for 2–3 minutes until the mixture becomes tacky and evenly seasoned.","Cook a teaspoon of the mixture in a small pan and taste it; adjust the fish sauce or curry paste before stuffing the full batch.","Cover the sausage mixture and refrigerate for at least 1 hour so the flavors meld and the meat stays cold.","Rinse and prepare the sausage casings according to their package instructions.","Stuff the mixture loosely into the casings, avoiding air pockets and leaving enough room for expansion. Coil or link the sausage without packing it too tightly.","Prick only obvious trapped air bubbles with a clean needle or sausage pricker.","Cook over medium, indirect grill heat or in a moderate oven for about 20–25 minutes, turning regularly so the casing browns evenly without splitting.","Continue until the center of the sausage is fully cooked and the exterior is browned and aromatic.","Rest 5 minutes before slicing, then serve with fresh vegetables or sticky rice."],"th_017":["Soak the dried chilies until pliable, then pound them with shallots, garlic, and shrimp paste into a coarse but cohesive paste.","Chop the tomatoes and have the ground pork, fish sauce, sugar, and cilantro ready before heating the skillet.","Heat the skillet over medium heat and fry the chile paste for about 2 minutes, stirring, until fragrant and slightly darkened.","Add the ground pork and break it into fine crumbles. Cook 5–7 minutes, until fully cooked with no raw pink meat remaining.","Add the chopped tomatoes and stir well, scraping up any paste from the pan.","Lower the heat and simmer 10–12 minutes, stirring occasionally and crushing the tomatoes, until they collapse into a thick, spoonable relish.","Season with fish sauce and sugar a little at a time, keeping the dip savory and tomato-forward.","Remove from the heat, finish with cilantro, and serve warm with raw or blanched vegetables."],"th_018":["Roast the green chilies, shallots, and garlic over a grill, broiler, or dry skillet for 10–15 minutes, turning regularly, until softened and charred in spots.","Transfer the roasted vegetables to a bowl and cover loosely for about 5 minutes so the skins loosen.","When cool enough to handle, peel away only the loosest blackened skins; keep some char for the smoky flavor.","Pound the roasted chilies, shallots, and garlic in a mortar into a coarse relish rather than a smooth puree.","Season with fish sauce and lime juice, mixing and tasting until the dip is salty, smoky, hot, and bright.","Fold in cilantro just before serving.","Serve at room temperature with fresh or blanched vegetables and sticky rice."],"th_019":["Cut the pork belly or shoulder into large bite-size chunks. Toss with the hang-lay curry paste and curry powder and let stand for about 15 minutes.","Heat a heavy pot over medium-high heat and brown the pork for 5–6 minutes, turning so several sides take on color.","Add the water, tamarind concentrate, fish sauce, palm sugar, sliced ginger, and garlic. Stir well and scrape up any browned bits.","Bring to a simmer, cover partially, and cook very gently for about 75 minutes, stirring occasionally.","Check the liquid level during the braise and add a small splash of hot water if the pot is drying before the pork becomes tender.","Add the peanuts and continue cooking uncovered for another 15–20 minutes, until the pork is very tender and the sauce has reduced to a glossy coating.","Taste and adjust with tamarind for sourness, fish sauce for salt, or palm sugar for a restrained sweetness.","Skim excess fat if desired and let the curry rest 5–10 minutes before serving.","Serve with hot rice."],"th_020":["Shred the green papaya into thin, crisp strands. Cut the long beans into short lengths and halve the cherry tomatoes.","Pound the garlic and Thai chilies lightly in the mortar until broken down but not completely smooth.","Add the long beans and bruise them with a few firm strikes so they soften slightly without turning mushy.","Add the tomatoes and press gently with the pestle just enough to release some juice.","Add the fish sauce, lime juice, and palm sugar. Stir and pound lightly until the sugar dissolves into the dressing.","Add the shredded green papaya and use a spoon and pestle together to lift, toss, and lightly bruise it until every strand is coated but still crisp.","Taste and adjust the sour-salty-sweet-hot balance before adding the peanuts.","Fold in the roasted peanuts at the very end and serve immediately."],"th_021":["Thinly slice the shallots and scallions, pick the mint and cilantro leaves, and have the toasted rice powder, lime juice, fish sauce, and chile flakes ready.","Put the ground chicken in a skillet with a few tablespoons of water and cook over medium heat, breaking it into small crumbles.","Cook for about 6–8 minutes, stirring, until fully cooked but still moist; do not brown it heavily.","Remove from the heat and let the chicken cool for about 2 minutes so the fresh herbs will not wilt immediately.","Stir in the lime juice, fish sauce, and chile flakes while the chicken is still warm.","Fold in the shallots, scallions, toasted rice powder, mint, and cilantro.","Taste and adjust so the larb is brightly sour, salty, spicy, and aromatic, with the toasted rice adding a nutty texture.","Serve immediately with lettuce, cabbage, or sticky rice."],"th_022":["Season the pork neck or shoulder lightly and heat the grill to medium-high.","Grill the pork for about 5–7 minutes per side, depending on thickness, until deeply browned and fully cooked while remaining juicy.","Transfer to a board and rest for 5–8 minutes so the juices redistribute.","While the pork rests, combine fish sauce, lime juice, chile flakes, and toasted rice powder in a mixing bowl.","Slice the pork thinly across the grain and add it to the dressing while still warm.","Add the sliced shallots and scallions and toss thoroughly so the meat picks up the dressing.","Fold in the mint and cilantro only at the end so the leaves stay fresh.","Taste for a sour-salty-spicy balance and serve warm with sticky rice."],"th_023":["Pound the garlic, coriander roots, and white pepper into a smooth paste.","Mix the paste with fish sauce, light soy sauce, palm sugar, and coconut milk until the sugar dissolves.","Score thick chicken pieces lightly if needed, then coat every piece thoroughly in the marinade.","Cover and refrigerate for at least 3 hours, turning once if possible so the seasoning stays even.","Heat the grill for medium, mostly indirect cooking. Remove the chicken from the refrigerator while the grill heats so the surface loses some of its chill.","Grill the chicken for 30–40 minutes, turning regularly and moving pieces away from flare-ups so the sugary marinade browns without burning.","Check the thickest pieces for complete doneness; the center should be fully cooked and the juices clear.","Move the chicken briefly over stronger heat at the end if more char is desired.","Rest for 5 minutes before serving.","Serve with sticky rice and a dipping sauce."],"th_024":["Rinse the Thai glutinous rice several times until the water is mostly clear.","Cover with plenty of cool water and soak for at least 4 hours or overnight; the grains need to hydrate before steaming.","Drain the soaked rice thoroughly and spread it evenly in a sticky-rice basket or a cheesecloth-lined steamer.","Bring the steamer water to a steady boil, making sure the water does not touch the rice.","Steam for about 20 minutes, keeping the lid on so the steam stays concentrated.","Turn or flip the rice mass so the top and bottom cook evenly, then steam another 8–10 minutes.","Check that the grains are translucent, tender, and chewy with no hard center.","Transfer to a basket or bowl, fluff lightly, and rest covered for 5 minutes before serving so the steam redistributes."],"th_025":["Mix fish sauce, oyster sauce, light soy sauce, palm sugar, white pepper, and minced garlic until the sugar dissolves.","Coat the pork neck thoroughly with the marinade, cover, and refrigerate for at least 90 minutes.","Heat the grill to medium-high. Remove excess wet marinade from the pork so it browns rather than scorching.","Grill for about 6–8 minutes per side, depending on thickness, turning once or twice for even color.","Continue cooking until the exterior is deeply browned and the center is fully cooked but still juicy.","Transfer to a board and rest for 5–10 minutes.","Slice thinly across the grain so the rich pork neck stays tender.","Serve with a lime-and-chile dipping sauce and sticky rice."],"th_026":["Pound the garlic, coriander roots, and white pepper into a smooth paste.","Mix the paste with fish sauce, light soy sauce, and sugar. Coat the chicken pieces thoroughly, cover, and refrigerate for at least 2 hours.","Remove the chicken from the refrigerator about 15–20 minutes before frying so the surface chill lessens. Keep it covered and do not leave it out for an extended period.","Mix the rice flour through the marinated chicken just before frying so every piece has a thin, even coating rather than a heavy batter.","Heat enough oil in a deep skillet or pot to fry safely. Bring it to about 170–175°C.","Lower the chicken into the oil in batches without crowding. Fry 12–16 minutes depending on piece size, turning several times for even browning.","Monitor the oil temperature between batches so the coating becomes crisp without burning before the center cooks.","Check the thickest chicken pieces for complete doneness before removing them from the oil.","Drain on a rack so steam does not soften the crust.","Serve hot with fried shallots and sticky rice."],"th_027":["Very finely slice the lemongrass and makrut lime leaves so the fibrous herbs disappear into the finished stir-fry.","Heat a wok over medium-high heat and add the ground pork or chicken.","Cook for 5–7 minutes, breaking it into small dry crumbles, until fully cooked and much of its moisture has evaporated.","Add the southern curry paste and stir-fry for 2–3 minutes so it coats the meat and loses its raw smell.","Add the sliced lemongrass and makrut lime leaves and toss for about 1 minute, until intensely aromatic.","Season with fish sauce and palm sugar, using the sugar sparingly; kua kling should be savory, hot, and fairly dry.","Continue tossing over medium heat for another 1–2 minutes until no loose liquid remains and the curry paste clings to the meat.","Serve immediately with jasmine rice and fresh vegetables."],"th_028":["Pound the garlic and black peppercorns into a coarse aromatic paste. Cut the pork belly or shoulder into large, even chunks.","Heat a heavy pot over medium heat and cook the garlic-pepper paste briefly until fragrant.","Add the pork and cook for 5–6 minutes, turning, until lightly browned and coated in the aromatics.","Add the dark soy sauce, light soy sauce, palm sugar, star anise, cinnamon, and water. Stir and scrape up any browned bits.","Bring to a simmer, cover partially, and braise gently for about 75 minutes.","Turn the pork occasionally and check the liquid level; add a small splash of hot water only if needed to prevent scorching before the meat becomes tender.","Uncover and simmer another 15–20 minutes, until the pork is very tender and the braising liquid has reduced to a dark, glossy sauce.","Taste and adjust the soy-sugar balance, keeping the dish deeply savory rather than candy-sweet.","Remove the star anise and cinnamon and skim excess fat if desired.","Rest for 5 minutes, then serve with rice."],"th_029":["Cool the cooked jasmine rice completely and loosen the grains so they are not clumped.","Very finely slice the lemongrass and prepare the bean sprouts, shredded green mango, carrot, and mixed herbs. Toast the coconut if it is not already toasted.","Stir the seasoned fish sauce, lime juice, and palm sugar together until the sugar dissolves. Taste and adjust the dressing before it touches the rice.","Arrange the rice in a large bowl or platter with separate piles of lemongrass, sprouts, mango, carrot, herbs, and toasted coconut.","Keep the dressing separate until the moment of serving so the herbs and vegetables stay crisp.","Drizzle over enough dressing to season the rice without making it wet.","Toss thoroughly at the table so every bite includes rice, herbs, fruit, vegetables, and coconut.","Taste once more and add a little extra lime or fish sauce if needed, then serve immediately."],"th_030":["Pound the fresh turmeric, garlic, coriander roots, and white pepper into a smooth, vivid yellow paste.","Mix the paste with fish sauce, soy sauce, and palm sugar until the sugar dissolves.","Coat the chicken pieces thoroughly, working some marinade into any scored or jointed areas. Cover and refrigerate for at least 2 hours.","Heat a grill for medium, mostly indirect cooking, or preheat the oven to a moderate roasting temperature.","Cook the chicken for about 30–35 minutes, turning several times on the grill or once during roasting so it colors evenly.","Move pieces away from very strong heat if the turmeric-garlic coating begins to darken before the center is cooked.","Check the thickest chicken pieces for complete doneness.","Rest the cooked chicken for 5 minutes.","Serve with rice and fresh vegetables."],"th_031":["Score the prepared whole fish lightly through the thickest parts so it cooks evenly, then place it on a heatproof plate that fits the steamer.","Bring the steamer water to a strong boil before adding the fish.","Steam the fish for about 10–15 minutes, depending on thickness, without repeatedly lifting the lid.","Check the thickest flesh near the backbone; it should be opaque, moist, and flake cleanly when fully cooked.","While the fish steams, finely chop the garlic and Thai chilies.","Stir the garlic, chilies, lime juice, fish sauce, sugar, and warm stock together until the sugar dissolves. Taste for a sharp sour-salty-hot balance.","Carefully pour off excess steaming liquid from the fish plate if it is watery.","Spoon the lime-garlic dressing over the fish while it is still very hot.","Finish with cilantro and serve immediately."],"th_032":["Soak the glass noodles in room-temperature water until flexible, then drain very well and cut them into manageable lengths if needed.","Bruise the ginger, garlic, and cilantro roots and spread them over the bottom of a heavy lidded pot.","Mix the oyster sauce, soy sauce, fish sauce, sugar, and stock until the sugar dissolves.","Toss the drained glass noodles with the seasoned stock so the strands are evenly coated.","Layer the seasoned noodles over the aromatics and arrange the shrimp on top.","Cover tightly and cook over medium to medium-low heat for about 8 minutes without repeatedly opening the lid.","Open, turn the noodles gently, and continue covered for another 2–4 minutes, until the noodles are tender and the shrimp are pink, opaque, and fully cooked.","If the noodles are still firm but the pot is dry, add a small splash of hot stock and steam briefly rather than overcooking the shrimp.","Scatter over scallions, rest covered for 1 minute, and serve straight from the pot."],"th_033":["Rinse the jasmine rice and place it in a heavy saucepan with the chicken stock.","Bring to a boil, then lower to a gentle simmer and cook for about 40–45 minutes, stirring the bottom periodically, until the grains break down and the porridge becomes creamy.","While the rice cooks, season the ground pork lightly and form it into small rough meatballs or teaspoon-size pieces.","Add the pork to the simmering congee and cook for 6–8 minutes, stirring gently so the pieces remain distinct, until fully cooked.","Add sliced or grated ginger and season gradually with fish sauce.","If using eggs, crack them into the gently simmering porridge and cook to your preferred set; use fully cooked eggs when appropriate for the diner.","Adjust the consistency with hot stock if the congee becomes thicker than you like.","Ladle into bowls and finish with white pepper, scallions, and cilantro.","Serve very hot."],"th_034":["Flake the cooked white fish carefully and check for any bones. Bruise the lemongrass and tear the makrut lime leaves.","Cook the thick coconut cream over medium heat until glossy.","Add the red curry paste and fry for 2–3 minutes, stirring, until fragrant and the raw paste smell is gone.","Add the cooked fish and stir it through the curry base, breaking it into fine flakes so it becomes part of the sauce rather than remaining in large chunks.","Pour in the remaining coconut milk and add the lemongrass and makrut lime leaves.","Simmer gently for 15–20 minutes, stirring often enough to prevent scorching, until the sauce is aromatic and slightly thickened.","Season with fish sauce and palm sugar, keeping the sauce savory and fragrant rather than sweet.","Prepare the khanom jeen noodles separately according to the package or fresh-noodle instructions, then drain well.","Divide noodles among plates or bowls and spoon over the hot fish curry.","Serve with boiled eggs and plenty of fresh vegetables so diners can add them at the table."],"th_035":["Blend the rice flour, cooked jasmine rice, coconut milk, water, sugar, and salt until the batter is smooth. Let it stand for about 15 minutes so the flour hydrates.","Stir the thick coconut cream with the topping sugar in a separate bowl.","Prepare any optional scallion, corn, or taro toppings in small pieces so they cook quickly.","Heat a khanom krok pan or takoyaki-style pan over medium-low heat and lightly grease each well.","Stir the batter, then fill each well about three-quarters full.","Spoon a small amount of the sweet coconut topping into the center of each cake and add any optional topping.","Cover and cook for 5–7 minutes, rotating the pan if necessary, until the edges are crisp and browned while the centers are softly set.","Use a small spoon or pick to lift the cakes carefully; pair two halves together if you prefer the traditional presentation.","Serve warm while the edges are still crisp."],"th_036":["Finely slice the makrut lime leaves and scallions. If using fresh corn, cut the kernels from the cob and break up any large clusters.","Mix the corn, rice flour, egg, red curry paste, lime leaf, fish sauce, and scallions into a thick batter.","Let the batter rest for about 10 minutes so the rice flour hydrates; it should hold together on a spoon without being runny.","Heat a shallow layer of neutral oil over medium heat.","Drop small spoonfuls of batter into the oil without crowding the skillet and flatten them slightly.","Fry for 2–3 minutes per side, until crisp and golden and the egg-and-flour center is fully set.","Transfer to a rack or paper-lined tray and let excess oil drain briefly.","Serve hot with sweet chile sauce or cucumber relish."],"th_037":["Beat the eggs vigorously with fish sauce and optional lime juice until the mixture is foamy and uniform. Stir in the scallions.","Pour enough neutral oil into a wok or small skillet to create a generous shallow layer and heat it over medium-high heat until shimmering.","Give the eggs one final vigorous whisk immediately before cooking to restore the foam.","Pour the egg mixture into the hot oil from slightly above the pan; the edges should puff and bubble immediately.","Cook for about 1–2 minutes, until the underside is deeply golden and the center is mostly set.","Turn the omelette carefully and cook the second side for another 30–60 seconds, until fully set.","Lift out and drain briefly.","Serve immediately over hot jasmine rice while the edges are still crisp."],"th_038":["Rinse the Thai glutinous rice until the water is mostly clear, then soak it in plenty of cool water for at least 2 hours.","Drain thoroughly and steam the rice over boiling water for about 25–30 minutes, turning once if necessary, until tender, translucent, and chewy with no hard center.","While the rice steams, warm the coconut milk, sugar, and salt over low heat just until the sugar dissolves; do not boil it hard.","Reserve a small portion of the coconut mixture for serving.","Transfer the hot steamed rice to a bowl and immediately fold in most of the warm coconut mixture.","Cover and let the rice rest for about 20 minutes so it absorbs the coconut seasoning evenly.","Peel and slice the ripe mangoes only shortly before serving.","Spoon the coconut sticky rice onto plates, arrange mango alongside, drizzle with the reserved coconut sauce, and add toasted mung beans or sesame if using."],"th_039":["Dice the water chestnuts into small even cubes. Tint them lightly with food coloring or beet juice if using, then drain away excess liquid.","Toss the damp water-chestnut pieces thoroughly in tapioca starch until every side is coated, then shake off loose excess starch.","Bring a saucepan of water to a gentle boil and add the coated pieces in batches so they do not clump.","Cook for about 2–3 minutes, until the ruby pieces float and the starch coating turns translucent.","Lift them immediately into cold water to stop the cooking and keep the coating springy; drain when cool.","Warm the coconut milk, sugar, and salt over low heat just until the sugar dissolves, then cool completely.","Chill the coconut mixture if you prefer the dessert very cold.","Divide crushed ice among bowls, add the ruby pieces and optional young coconut or jackfruit, and pour over the sweet coconut milk.","Serve immediately before the ice dilutes the dessert too much."],"th_040":["Bring the water to a full boil, remove it from the strongest heat, and add the Thai tea mix or strong black tea.","Steep for about 5–6 minutes; steeping much longer can make the tea unnecessarily bitter.","Strain the tea thoroughly while it is still hot.","Stir in the sugar and sweetened condensed milk until completely dissolved.","Let the sweetened tea cool for about 20 minutes, then chill further if time allows so it will not immediately melt all the serving ice.","Fill tall glasses generously with ice and pour in the tea.","Float evaporated milk over the top of each glass.","Serve immediately and stir before drinking."]};
const apply=(rows)=>{for(const row of (rows||[])){const id=String(row?.[0]||'');const steps=METHODS[id];if(steps)row[13]=steps.slice();}};
apply(window.BENTO_CN);
apply(window.BENTO_THAI_ROWS);
window.BENTO_METHOD_DEPTH_VERSION=26;
})();
